Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 12/17/2024]
1. Er = 208 keV: not clear how the uncertainty, Ex = 5711.4 +- 0.7 keV, was obtained in ENSDF 2015; the measured values scatter by several keV [see Table III in
   Matic et al. 2009]; we increase the uncertainty to 2 keV.
2. We disregard the level at Ex = 5838 keV listed in ENSDF 2015; the existence of this state has not been confirmed by any other experiment.
3. Er = 451 keV: we increase the uncertainty to 2 keV [similar comment to footnote 1. above].
4. Er = 740 keV: we calculated the resonance energy assuming Ex = 6242.7 keV; however, ENSDF 2015 states that this is doubtful; in Matic et al. 2009, this state is
   assumed to be located at 6254 keV; note that our adopted resonance energy is in agreement with the directly measured value by D'Auria et al. 2004; to account for
   an additional energy bias, we increase the uncertainty to 5 keV.
5. Er = 823, 1105 keV: again, we increase the uncertainty to 5 keV, based on the observed scatter of Ex values listed in Table III in Matic et al. 2009; the proton
   and gamma-ray partial widths are estimated from the measured resonance strengths and total widths.
6. All resonance strengths are adopted from the direct radioactive ion beam measurement of D'Auria et al. 2004.
7. Direct capture: adopted C2S values are the average of experimental results (evaluated in Endt 1977) and shell-model calculations (Smirnova & Coc 2000).
